==== The Last General Direction: Keep the Word of Christ’s Patience

#smallcaps[The] directions dwelt on in the previous chapters are partly given to us, in their various particulars, here and there in the Scripture, and partly arise from the nature of the thing itself. One general direction remains, which takes in all that went before and adds many more particulars to them. It contains a proven antidote against the poison of temptation, a remedy that Christ himself has marked with a seal of efficacy and success. It is given us in Rev 3:10, in the words of our Savior himself to the church of Philadelphia: “Because you have kept the word of My patience,” he says, “I will also keep you from the hour of temptation that is about to come upon the whole world, to test those who dwell on the earth.” Christ is “the same yesterday and today and forever.” As he dealt with the church of Philadelphia, so he will deal with us. If we “keep the word of his patience,” he will “keep us from the hour of temptation.” Since, then, this is a way of rolling the whole care of this weighty matter on him who is able to bear it, it calls for our special consideration.

So I will show (1.) what it is to “keep the word of Christ’s patience,” so that we may know how to do our duty; and (2.) how this will be a means of our preservation, which will establish us in the faith of Christ’s promise.

(1.) The word of Christ is the word of the gospel: the word he revealed from the bosom of the Father; the word of the Word; the word spoken in time by the eternal Word. So it is called “the word of Christ” (Col 3:16), or “the gospel of Christ” (Rom 1:16; 1 Cor 9:12), and “the doctrine of Christ” (Heb 6:1). It is “of Christ” as its author (Heb 1:1, 2), and as concerning him, its chief subject or matter (2 Cor 1:20). Now, this word is called “the word of Christ’s patience,” or tolerance and forbearance, on account of the patience and long-suffering that the Lord Christ exercises toward the world, and toward all persons in it, in administering it. He does so both actively and passively, in bearing with men and in enduring things from them:

\[1.\] He is patient toward his #emph[saints]\; he bears with them, and suffers from them. He “is patient with you” (2 Pet 3:9), that is, with those who believe. The gospel is the word of Christ’s patience even to believers. A soul acquainted with the gospel knows that no attribute of Christ is made more glorious in it than his patience. He bears with so many unkindnesses, so many groundless breaches, so many neglects of his love, so many affronts to his grace, so many violations of commitments; and this shows his gospel to be not only the word of his grace, but also the word of his patience. He also suffers #emph[from] them in all the reproaches they bring on his name and ways; and he suffers #emph[in] them, for “in all their distress, He too was afflicted.”

\[2.\] Toward his #emph[elect] who are not yet effectually called. He stands waiting at the door of their hearts and knocks to be let in (Rev 3:20). He deals with them by every means, and still stands and waits until “My head is drenched with dew, my hair with the dampness of the night” (Song 5:2), enduring the cold and discomforts of the night so that he may be let in when his morning comes. Often for a long season they scorn him in his person, persecute him in his saints and ways, and revile him in his word, while he stands at the door in the word of his patience, with his heart full of love toward their poor rebellious souls.

\[3.\] Toward the #emph[perishing world]. That is why the time of his kingdom in this world is called the time of his “patience” (Rev 1:9). He “bore with great patience the vessels of His wrath” (Rom 9:22). As long as the gospel is administered in the world, he is patient toward its men, until the saints in heaven and earth are astonished and cry out, “How long?” (Ps 13:1, 2; Rev 6:10). And the men of the world themselves mock him as if he were an idol (2 Pet 3:4). He endures bitter things from them in his name, ways, worship, saints, promises, and threats, in all that concerns his honor and love; and yet he passes them by, lets them alone, and does them good. Nor will he cut short this way of dealing until the gospel is preached no more. Patience must go with the gospel.

Now, this is the word that is to be kept, so that we may be kept from “the hour of temptation.”

(2.) Three things are implied in keeping this word: 1. knowledge; 2. valuing it; 3. obedience.

\[1.\] #emph[Knowledge]. The man who will keep this word must know it and be acquainted with it in four ways: 1st, as a word of #emph[grace] and #emph[mercy], to save him; 2dly, as a word of #emph[holiness] and #emph[purity], to sanctify him; 3dly, as a word of #emph[liberty] and #emph[power], to ennoble him and set him free; 4thly, as a word of #emph[consolation], to support him in every condition.

1st. As a word of #emph[grace] and #emph[mercy], able to save us. “It is the power of God for salvation” (Rom 1:16); “the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation” (Titus 2:11); “the word of His grace, which can build you#super[+] up and give you#super[+] an inheritance among all who are sanctified” (Acts 20:32); “the word … which can save your#super[+] souls” (Jas 1:21). When the word of the gospel is known as a word of mercy, grace, and pardon, as the only evidence for life, as the deed that conveys an eternal inheritance; when the soul finds it to be such to itself, it will strive to keep it.

2dly. As a word of #emph[holiness] and #emph[purity], able to sanctify him. “You#super[+] are already clean because of the word I have spoken to you#super[+],” says our Savior (John 15:3). His prayer in chapter 17:17 is to the same end. The man who does not know the word of Christ’s patience as a sanctifying, cleansing word, in its power on his own soul, neither knows it nor keeps it. The empty profession of our days does not know one step toward this duty; and that is why most are so overpowered by temptations. Men full of self, of the world, of fury, ambition, and almost all unclean lusts, still talk of keeping the word of Christ! See 1 Pet 1:2; 2 Tim 2:19.

3dly. As a word of #emph[liberty] and #emph[power], to ennoble him and set him free. It sets him free not only from the guilt of sin and from wrath, for it does that as a word of grace and mercy; and not only from the power of sin, for it does that as a word of holiness; but also from all outward regard for men or the world that might entangle or enslave him. It declares us to be Christ’s freemen, in bondage to no one (John 8:32; 1 Cor 7:23). It does not free us from due subjection to superiors, nor from any duty, nor does it free us to any sin (1 Pet 2:16). But in two respects it is a word of freedom, liberty, largeness of mind, power, and deliverance from bondage:

(1st.) With respect to #emph[conscience] in the worship of God (Gal 5:1).

(2dly.) With respect to #emph[ignoble], slavish regard for the men or things of the world in the course of our pilgrimage. The gospel gives a free, large, and noble spirit, in subjection to God and to no one else. In it is given a spirit “not … of fear, but of power, love, and a sound mind” (2 Tim 1:7), a mind “without being frightened in any way” (Phil 1:28), not swayed by any side interest whatever. Nothing is more unworthy of the gospel than a mind in bondage to persons or things, prostituting itself to the lusts of men or the frights of the world. And the man who knows the word of Christ’s patience in this way, really and in power, is by that very knowledge freed from countless, unspeakable temptations.

4thly. As a word of #emph[consolation], to support him in every condition and to be a full portion when he lacks everything. It is a word that comes with “an inexpressible and glorious joy.” It gives support, relief, refreshment, satisfaction, peace, consolation, joy, boasting, and glory in every condition whatever. To know the word of Christ’s patience in this way, to know the gospel in this way, is the first part, and a great part, of this condition of our preservation from the hour and power of temptation.

\[2.\] #emph[Valuing] what is known in this way belongs to the keeping of this word. It is to be kept as a treasure. Τὴν καλὴν παρακαταθήκην, that excellent “depositum” \[deposit, the thing entrusted\], that is, the word of the gospel: “Keep the treasure entrusted to you,” says the apostle, “with the help of the Holy Spirit” (2 Tim 1:14). And, “hold firmly to the faithful word” (Titus 1:9). It is a good treasure, a faithful word; hold it firmly. It is a word that takes in the whole interest of Christ in the world. To value that as our chief treasure is to keep the word of Christ’s patience. Those who would have Christ’s regard in the time of temptation are not to be regardless of the things that concern him.

\[3.\] #emph[Obedience]. Personal obedience, in the universal keeping of all the commands of Christ, is the keeping of his word (John 14:15). Close adherence to Christ in holiness and universal obedience, at the very time when the opposition the gospel of Christ meets in the world makes it notably the word of his patience, is the life and soul of the duty required.

Now, all these are to be carried on with such intensity of mind and spirit, such care of heart, and such diligence of the whole person, as to make up a keeping of this word; and it evidently includes all these considerations.

We have arrived, then, at the sum of this safeguarding duty, this condition of freedom from the power of temptation. Suppose a man is duly acquainted with the gospel in its excellences, as a word of mercy, holiness, liberty, and consolation to him. He values it, in all that concerns it, as his choicest and only treasure. He makes it his business and the work of his life to give himself up to it in universal obedience, and especially when opposition and apostasy try the patience of Christ to the utmost. That man will be preserved from the hour of temptation.

This takes in all that went before, and it excludes all other ways of reaching the end in view. Let no man think that without this he will be kept for one hour from entering into temptation; wherever he fails in it, there temptation enters. That this will be a sure preservative may appear from the following considerations:

(1.) It has the #emph[promise] of preservation, and this alone has it. It is solemnly promised on this account to the church of Philadelphia, in the place mentioned. When a great trial and temptation was to come on the world at the opening of the seventh seal, a charge is given for the preservation of God’s sealed ones (Rev 7:3), and they are described as those who keep the word of Christ; for the promise is that it should be so.

Now, in every promise there are three things to be considered: \[1.\] the #emph[faithfulness] of the Father, who gives it; \[2.\] the #emph[grace] of the Son, which is its matter; \[3.\] the #emph[power] and #emph[efficacy] of the Holy Spirit, who puts the promise into effect. And all these are engaged for the preservation of such persons from the hour of temptation.

\[1.\] The #emph[faithfulness] of God goes with the promise. Our deliverance is rested on this account (1 Cor 10:13). Though we are tempted, yet we will be kept from the hour of temptation; it will not grow too strong for us. What comes on us we will be able to bear, and what would be too hard for us we will escape. But what assurance do we have of this? Even the faithfulness of God: “God is faithful; He will not let you#super[+] …,” and so on. And in what is God’s faithfulness seen and exercised? “He who promised is faithful” (Heb 10:23); his faithfulness consists in his fulfilling his promises. “He remains faithful, for He cannot deny Himself” (2 Tim 2:13). So by being under the promise we have the faithfulness of God engaged for our preservation.

\[2.\] In every promise of the covenant there is #emph[the grace of the Son]\; that is the subject matter of all the promises. “I will keep you.” How? “By my grace with you.” So whatever help the grace of Christ can give, a soul that has a right to this promise will enjoy it in the hour of temptation. Paul’s temptation rose very high; it was likely to have come to its prevailing hour. He “pleaded with the Lord,” that is, the Lord Jesus Christ, for help (2 Cor 12:8), and received this answer from him: “My grace is sufficient for you” (verse 9). That it was the Lord Christ and his grace he had especially to deal with is evident from the close of that verse: “I will boast all the more gladly in my weaknesses, so that the power of Christ may rest on me,” or, so that the efficacy of the grace of Christ in preserving me may be made evident. So too Heb 2:18.

\[3.\] The #emph[efficacy] of the Spirit goes with the promises. He is called “the Holy Spirit of promise,” not only because he is promised by Christ, but also because he effectually makes good the promise and brings it to fulfillment in our souls. He too, then, is engaged to preserve the soul that walks according to the rule laid down. See Isa 59:21. So where the promise is, there is all this help. The faithfulness of the Father, the grace of the Son, and the power of the Spirit are all engaged in our preservation.

(2.) This #emph[constant, universal] keeping of Christ’s word of patience will keep the heart and soul in such a frame that no prevailing temptation, by virtue of any advantages whatever, can seize on it so as to prevail against it totally. So David prays, “May integrity and uprightness preserve me” (Ps 25:21). This integrity and uprightness is the Old Testament’s keeping of the word of Christ: universal, close walking with God. Now, how can they preserve a man? Why, by keeping his heart in such a frame, so defended on every side, that no evil can approach him or take hold of him. Let a man fail in his integrity, and he has an open place for temptation to enter (Isa 57:21). To keep the word of Christ is to keep it universally, as has been shown. This exercises grace in all the faculties of the soul and surrounds it with the full armor of God. The understanding is full of light; the affections are full of love and holiness. Let the wind blow from whatever quarter it will, the soul is fenced and fortified. Let the enemy assault when he pleases, or by what means he pleases, everything in the soul of such a man is on guard; “How could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” is at hand. Deliverance and security arise from this source especially on two accounts:

\[1.\] By the #emph[mortification] of the heart to the matter of temptations. The prevailing of any temptation comes from this: the heart is ready to join with its matter. There are lusts within suited to the proposals of the world or of Satan from without. That is why James traces all temptations back to our own lusts (1:14), because temptations either proceed from them or are made effectual by them, as has been declared. Why do terror or threats turn us aside from due constancy in doing our duty? Is it not because there is unmortified, fleshly fear remaining in us, which raises a tumult in such a season? Why do the allurements of the world and compromises with men entangle us? Is it not because our affections are entangled with the things and considerations set before us? Now, keeping the word of Christ’s patience in the way declared keeps the heart mortified to these things, and so it is not easily entangled by them. The apostle says, “I have been crucified with Christ” (Gal 2:20). The man who keeps close to Christ is crucified with him, and is dead to all the desires of the flesh and the world; as it is more fully in chapter 6:14. Here the match is broken off, and all love, all entangling love, is dissolved. The heart is crucified to the world and all things in it. Now, the matter of almost all temptations is taken from the world; its men or its things make them up. “As to these things,” says the apostle (and so it is with everyone who keeps the word of Christ), “I am crucified to them. My heart is mortified to them. I have no desire for them, no affection toward them, and no delight in them; and they are crucified to me. The crowns, glories, thrones, pleasures, and profits of the world: I see nothing desirable in them. The lusts and sensual pleasures, the love, regard, and honors of men, name and reputation among them: they are all a thing of nothing. I have no value or esteem for them.” This soul is safeguarded from the assaults of many temptations. When Achan saw “a beautiful cloak from Babylon, two hundred shekels of silver, and a bar of gold,” first he “coveted them,” and then he “took them” (Josh 7:21). Temptation subtly spreads before men’s eyes the Babylonian cloak of favor, praise, and peace, the silver of pleasure or profit, and the golden contentments of the flesh. If there is something alive and unmortified in them that will immediately fall to coveting, then whatever fear of punishment may follow, the heart or the hand will be put forth to iniquity.

This, then, is where the security of the frame described lies: it is always accompanied by a mortified heart, crucified to the things that are the matter of our temptations. Without this it is utterly impossible for us to be preserved for one moment when any temptation befalls us. If liking and love for the things offered, slipped in, and commended in the temptation are alive and active in us, we will not be able to resist and stand.

\[2.\] In this frame the heart is #emph[filled with better things] and their excellence, so far as to be fortified against the matter of any temptation. See what resolve this brings Paul to (Phil 3:8): all things are “loss” and “rubbish” to him. Who would go out of his way to get his arms full of loss and rubbish? And where does he get this estimate of the most desirable things in the world? From his precious estimate of the excellence of Christ. So in verse 10. When the soul is exercised in communion with Christ and in walking with him, it drinks new wine, and cannot desire the old things of the world, for it says, “The new is better.” It tastes every day how gracious the Lord is, and so it does not long for the sweetness of forbidden things, which indeed have none. The man who makes it his business to eat daily from the tree of life will have no appetite for other fruit, though the tree that bore it seems to stand in the middle of paradise. The bride makes this the means of her preservation: the excellence above all other desirable things that she found in Christ and his graces by daily communion. Let a soul exercise itself in communion with Christ in the good things of the gospel, pardon of sin, fruits of holiness, hope of glory, peace with God, joy in the Holy Spirit, dominion over sin, and it will have a mighty preservative against all temptations. As the full soul loathes honey, so a soul filled with fleshly, earthly, sensual contentments finds no relish or flavor in the sweetest spiritual things. In the same way, the man who is satisfied with the kindness of God as with marrow and fatness, who is entertained every day at the banquet of wine, of aged wine, finely refined, has a holy contempt for the baits and allurements that lie in prevailing temptations, and is safe.

(3.) The man who keeps the word of Christ’s patience in this way is always furnished with #emph[preserving considerations] and preserving principles, moral and real advantages for his preservation.

\[1.\] He is furnished with preserving #emph[considerations], which powerfully influence his soul in walking diligently with Christ. Besides the sense of duty that is always on him, he considers:

1st. How much Christ, whom his soul loves, is #emph[concerned] in him and in his careful walking. He considers that the presence of Christ is with him and his eye on him; that Christ weighs his heart and ways, as one greatly concerned in how he conducts himself in a time of trial. Christ shows that he does so in Rev 2:19–23. Christ considers everything, what is acceptable and what is to be rejected. The man knows that Christ is concerned for his own honor, that his name not be spoken evil of because of him. He knows that Christ is concerned in love for his soul, having this design on him, to “present” him “holy, unblemished, and blameless in His presence” (Col 1:22); and his Spirit is grieved where he is interrupted in this work. He knows that Christ is concerned on account of his gospel, its progress and acceptance in the world; its beauty would be smeared, its good things reviled, and its progress stopped, if such a man were overcome. And he knows that Christ is concerned in his love for others, who are grievously caused to stumble, and perhaps ruined, by the failures of such men. When Hymenaeus and Philetus fell, they overthrew the faith of some. So when intricate, perplexing, entangling temptations arise, public, private, or personal, such a soul, exercised in keeping the word of Christ’s patience, says: “Shall I now be careless? Shall I be negligent? Shall I go along with the world and its ways? Oh, what thoughts of heart he has concerning me, whose eye is on me! Shall I despise his honor, scorn his love, trample his gospel in the mud under the feet of men, and turn others aside from his ways? Shall a man like me flee, and give up resisting? It cannot be.” There is no man who keeps the word of the patience of Christ who is not full of this soul-pressing consideration. It dwells on his heart and spirit, and the love of Christ compels him to keep his heart and ways in this way (2 Cor 5:14).

2dly. The great consideration of #emph[the temptations of Christ] on his behalf, and of the victory Christ won in all assaults for his sake and his good, dwells on his spirit too. The prince of this world came upon him; the glory of this world was shown to him; everything in earth or hell that has either allurement or terror in it was set before him, to turn him aside from the work of mediation he had undertaken for us. He calls this whole life of his the time of his “temptations.” But he resisted all, conquered all, and has become a Captain of salvation to those who obey him. “And,” says the soul, “shall this temptation, these arguings, this plausible pretext, this sloth, this self-love, this sensuality, this bait of the world, turn me aside and prevail over me, so that I desert him who went before me, for my good, in the ways of all the temptations his holy nature was exposed to?”

3dly. #emph[Dark thoughts] of the #emph[loss of love], of the smiles of Christ’s face, also often exercise such a soul. He knows what it is to enjoy the favor of Christ, to have a sense of his love, to be accepted in his approaches to him, and to converse with him. And perhaps he has at times been at some loss in this, and so knows also what it is to be in the dark, at a distance from him. See how the bride behaves in such a case (Song 3:4). When she had found him again, she held him; she would not let him go; she would lose him no more.

\[2.\] The man who keeps the word of Christ’s patience has preserving #emph[principles] by which he is moved. Some of them may be mentioned:

1st. In all things he lives by #emph[faith], and is moved by it in all his ways (Gal 2:20). Now, faith, when put to use, has the power of preservation from temptation joined to it on two accounts:

(1st.) Because it empties the soul of its #emph[own wisdom], understanding, and fullness, so that it may act in the wisdom and fullness of Christ. The only counsel for preservation in trials and temptations lies in the wise man’s words, “Trust in the #smallcaps[Lord] with all your heart, and lean not on your own understanding” (Prov 3:5). This is the #emph[work] of faith; it #emph[is] faith; it is to #emph[live] by faith. The great failing of men in trials is their leaning toward, or leaning on, their own understanding and counsel. What is the outcome of it? “His vigorous stride is shortened, and his own counsel throws him down” (Job 18:7). First he is hampered, and then he is thrown down, and all by his own counsel, until he comes to be ashamed of them, as Ephraim was (Hos 10:6). Whenever in our trials we consult our own understandings and listen to our own reasonings, though they seem good and to tend toward our preservation, the principle of living by faith is stifled, and in the end we will be thrown down by our own counsels. Now, nothing can empty the heart of this self-fullness but faith: living by it, not living to ourselves, but having Christ live in us by our living by faith on him.

(2dly.) Faith, making the soul poor, empty, helpless, and destitute in itself, #emph[engages] the heart, will, and power of Jesus Christ for its help. I have spoken of this more fully elsewhere.

2dly. #emph[Love] for the saints, with care that they do not suffer on our account, is a great preserving principle in a time of temptations and trials. How powerful this was in David he declares in that earnest prayer: “May those who hope in You not be ashamed through me, O Lord #smallcaps[God] of Hosts; may those who seek You not be dishonored through me, O God of Israel” (Ps 69:6). That is, “Oh, let me not fail in such a way that those for whom I would lay down my life are put to shame, spoken evil of, dishonored, reviled, and despised on my account, for my failings.” A selfish soul, whose love is turned wholly inward, will never hold out in a time of trial.

Many other considerations and principles that go with those who keep the word of Christ’s patience in the way described above might be listed; but I will be content to have pointed to these.

And now will it be easy to determine why so many in our days are overcome in the time of trial, why the hour of temptation comes on them and bears them down before it, more or less? Is it not because, among the great multitude of professors we have, there are few who keep the word of the patience of Christ? If we willfully neglect or throw away our share in the promise of preservation, is it any wonder if we are not preserved? An hour of temptation has come upon the world to test those who dwell in it. It puts forth its power and efficacy in various ways. There is no way or thing in which it may not be seen working and showing itself. It appears in worldliness; in sensuality; in loose conduct; in neglect of spiritual duties, private and public; in foolish, loose, devilish opinions; in haughtiness and ambition; in envy and wrath; in strife and quarreling, revenge, and selfishness; in atheism and contempt of God. These are only branches of the same root, bitter streams of the same fountain, nourished by peace, prosperity, false security, the apostasies of professors, and the like. And, alas! how many fall daily under the power of this temptation in general! How few keep their garments girded about them and undefiled! And if some urgent, particular temptation befalls anyone, what instances have we of almost anyone escaping? May we not describe our condition as the apostle described that of the Corinthians under an outward visitation: “many among you are weak and sick, and a number of you have fallen asleep”? Some are wounded, some defiled, many utterly lost. What is the spring and fountain of this sad state of things? Is it not what has been said? We do not keep the word of Christ’s patience in universal, close walking with him, and so we lose the benefit of the promise given and joined to it.

If I set out to give instances of this, of professors falling short of keeping the word of Christ, it would be a long work. These four headings would take in most of them. First, #emph[conformity] to the world, from which Christ has redeemed us, in almost all things, with joy and delight in indiscriminate compromises with the men of the world. Secondly, #emph[neglect] of the duties Christ has commanded, from close meditation to public ordinances. Thirdly, #emph[strife], variance, and quarreling among ourselves, woeful judging and despising of one another on account of things foreign to the bond of communion between the saints. Fourthly, #emph[self-fullness] in principles and selfishness in ends. Now, where these things are, are men not fleshly? Is the word of Christ’s patience effectual in them? Will they be preserved? They will not.

Would you#super[+], then, be preserved and kept from the hour of temptation? Would you#super[+] watch against entering into it? Then take the following cautions, drawn from what has been set down in this chapter:

1\. Beware of leaning on deceitful helps, such as:

(1.) Your#super[+] own #emph[counsels], understandings, and reasonings. However plausibly you#super[+] argue with them in your#super[+] own defense, they will leave you#super[+] and betray you#super[+]. When the temptation rises to any height, they will all turn around, take the side of your#super[+] enemy, and plead as much for the matter of the temptation, whatever it is, as they pleaded before against its end and outcome.

(2.) The most vigorous efforts, by prayer, fasting, and other such means, against the #emph[particular lust], corruption, or temptation you#super[+] are exercised with and have to deal with. This will not help you#super[+] if in the meantime there are neglects on other accounts. A man may be heard wrestling, crying, and striving over some particular temptation, and then immediately fall into worldly ways, worldly compromises, looseness, and negligence in other things. It is righteous of Jesus Christ to leave such a man to the hour of temptation.

(3.) The general #emph[security] of the saints’ perseverance and preservation from total apostasy. Every guarantee God gives us is good in its kind and for the purpose for which it is given to us. But to use it for another end than the one it is given for is not good or profitable. To use the general assurance of preservation from total apostasy to hold up the spirit in the face of a particular temptation will not benefit the soul in the end; for in spite of that, this or that temptation may prevail. Many comfort themselves with this until they find themselves in the depth of perplexities.

2\. Apply yourselves to this great preservative, faithfully keeping the word of Christ’s patience in the midst of all trials and temptations:

(1.) In particular, consider wisely where the word of Christ’s patience is most likely to suffer in the days in which we live and the seasons that pass over us, and so set yourselves vigorously to keep it in that particular above all. You#super[+] will say, “How shall we know where the word of Christ’s patience is likely to suffer in any season?” I answer: consider what works he is especially doing in any season; neglect of his word with regard to them is where his word is likely to suffer. For instance, the works of Christ in which he has been especially engaged in our days and seasons seem to be these:

\[1.\] #emph[Pouring contempt] on the great men and great things of the world, with all its enjoyments. He has laid bare the nakedness of all earthly things by overturning, overturning, overturning both men and things, to make way for the things that cannot be shaken.

\[2.\] #emph[Owning the lot] of his own inheritance in a distinguishing way, putting a difference between the precious and the vile, and making his people dwell alone, not reckoned among the nations.

\[3.\] Being #emph[near] to faith and prayer, honoring them above all the strength and counsels of the sons of men.

\[4.\] Recovering his #emph[ordinances] and institutions from the fleshly administration they were in bondage under through the lusts of men, and bringing them forth in the beauty and the power of the Spirit.

In such a season, then, where must the special neglect of the word of Christ’s patience lie? Is it not in setting a value on the world and its things, which he has stained and trampled underfoot? Is it not in slighting his special lot, his people, and lumping them together with the men of the world? Is it not in leaning on our own counsels and understandings? Is it not in defiling his ordinances by giving the outer court of the temple to be trampled by unsanctified persons? Let us be watchful, then, and keep the word of the patience of Christ in these things, if we love our own preservation.

(2.) In this frame, press the Lord Jesus Christ with his blessed promises, and with all the considerations that may take and hold the King in his galleries, that may work on the heart of our blessed and merciful High Priest to give fitting help in time of need.
